{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses because it conflates 'methodological preference' with 'rational defensibility.' While Popper can prescribe preferring better-corroborated theories as a rule of thumb, the Problem explicitly asks if this preference is defensible *on his own terms* given that scientists (and Popper) act on it to navigate the world. The target admits the preference offers 'no guarantee of future success or proximity to truth.' If the preference provides no rational basis to expect the chosen theory to perform better than a rival in the next instance (since probability is rejected), then acting on it is indistinguishable from an arbitrary guess. Therefore, the preference smuggles in induction because any claim that it is 'defensible' implies a rational expectation of utility that Popper's framework explicitly forbids without inductive assumptions.","premise":"A preference is only rationally defensible if it provides a reason to expect the preferred option to yield better outcomes than the alternative; otherwise, it is merely an arbitrary choice.","successor_question":"If Popper admits the preference offers no expectation of future success, how can he distinguish his recommendation from advising scientists to flip a coin?","target":"013ff21c48935ba2325b560ec32b5aed85de59fe64cd39eb23b70020f165ef21"}]}